{"name":"Art Nouveau Violet","hex":"#a08994","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"art","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-5 language code for artificial languages."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The conscious production or arrangement of sounds, colours, forms, movements, or other elements in a manner that affects the senses and emotions, usually specifically the production of the beautiful in a graphic or plastic medium.","The creative and emotional expression of mental imagery, such as visual, auditory, social, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/art"},{"term":"nouveau","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["New, fashionable."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A wine released in the same year that the grapes were harvested to make it."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nouveau"},{"term":"violet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant or flower of the genus Viola, especially the fragrant Viola odorata;  similar-looking plants and flowers.","A person thought to resemble V. odorata, especially in its beauty and delicacy."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a violet colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/violet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Art Nouveau","blocks":[{"text":"Art Nouveau is an international style of art, architecture, and applied art, especially the decorative arts. It was often inspired by natural forms such as the sinuous curves of plants and flowers. Other characteristics of Art Nouveau were a sense of dynamism and movement, often given by asymmetry or whiplash lines, and the use of modern materials, particularly iron, glass, ceramics and later concrete, to create unusual forms and larger open spaces. It was popular between 1890 and 1910 during the Belle Époque period, and was a reaction against the academicism, eclecticism and historicism of 19th-century architecture and decorative art."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Abbesses.JPG/330px-Abbesses.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art_Nouveau"},{"title":"Art","blocks":[{"text":"Art is a diverse range of cultural activity centered around works utilizing creative or imaginative talents, which are expected to evoke a worthwhile experience, generally through an expression of emotional power, conceptual ideas, technical proficiency, or beauty."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Art-portrait-collage_2.jpg/330px-Art-portrait-collage_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art"},{"title":"Nouveau","blocks":[{"text":"A nouveau, or vin (de) primeur, is a wine which may be sold in the same year in which it was harvested."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nouveau"},{"title":"Violet (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Violet is the color of light at the short wavelength end of the visible spectrum. It is one of the seven colors that Isaac Newton labeled when dividing the spectrum of visible light in 1704. Violet light has a wavelength between approximately 380 and 435 nanometers. The color's name is derived from the Viola genus of flowers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Amatista_Laye_2.jpg/330px-Amatista_Laye_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Violet_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Otto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg/500px-Otto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Otto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg/500px-Otto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail"